[{:db/id 17592186341337 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)=\\langle x^3+2y^2,x^2+\\ln y\\rangle$ for $y>0$, and let $D$ be the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$ and $(1,1)$. For $0<\\epsilon<1$, let $C_\\epsilon$ be the positively oriented (counterclockwise) boundary of $D_\\epsilon=D\\cap\\{y\\ge\\epsilon\\}$. Use Green's theorem on $D_\\epsilon$ to evaluate the limiting circulation\n\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lim}}=\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\oint_{C_\\epsilon}F\\cdot dr.$$\n\nThis explicit one-sided limit is the intended interpretation. The original ordinary line integral over the untruncated triangle is undefined because $\\ln y$ is undefined on its entire bottom edge." :question/worked-solution "For each $0<\\epsilon<1$, the field is continuously differentiable on an open neighborhood of $D_\\epsilon$, since that region stays in $y>0$. Its positively oriented boundary has vertices $(\\epsilon,\\epsilon)$, $(2-\\epsilon,\\epsilon)$ and $(1,1)$. Green's theorem therefore applies there.\n\nWith $P=x^3+2y^2$ and $Q=x^2+\\ln y$,\n\n$$Q_x-P_y=2x-4y.$$\n\nThe truncated triangle is described by $\\epsilon\\le y\\le1$ and $y\\le x\\le2-y$. Hence\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{C_\\epsilon}F\\cdot dr&=\\int_\\epsilon^1\\int_y^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,dx\\,dy\\\\&=\\int_\\epsilon^1(4-12y+8y^2)\\,dy\\\\&=\\frac23-4\\epsilon+6\\epsilon^2-\\frac83\\epsilon^3.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTaking $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ gives $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lim}}=\\frac23$, the unchanged displayed option b.\n\nThe boundary interpretation also converges directly. The $\\ln y\\,dy$ term has primitive $H(y)=y\\ln y-y$, and $H(y)\\to0$ as $y\\downarrow0$. Each sloping side thus has its own finite one-sided integral; no cancellation of divergent integrals or principal-value convention is needed. On each truncated horizontal bottom edge, $dy=0$, while $F$ is defined because $y=\\epsilon>0$. The polynomial terms also converge. These facts establish the specified limiting circulation without assigning a value to $F$ on the original edge $y=0$ or claiming ordinary Green's theorem applies to the untruncated boundary." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
